@* AdminProducts.razor — Phase 3.3 (Round 6: server-driven grid, Radzen LoadData)

   Staff-facing product catalog management page at route `/Admin/Products`.
   Reached from the sidebar's "Admin → Manage Products" link (already wired
   in MainLayout.razor). Role-gated to Admin / Manager / Employee via
   [Authorize(Roles=...)] — customers and read-only users get a 403.

   PURPOSE:
     This page is the staff counterpart to the customer-facing /Products
     catalog. It exists so staff can:
       1. See ALL products in a compact, paginated table (mirrors AdminUsers).
       2. Add a new product → navigates to /Admin/Products/Create.
       3. Restock an existing product (IncreaseProductStockCommand).
       4. Deactivate a product (DeactivateProductCommand — sets StockQuantity
          to 0; see the command's XML doc for why this is the deactivation
          operation). Deactivation is gated by a popup that warns the user
          that the previous stock value will be lost.
       5. View a product's full details → /Products/{Id} (the customer
          detail page already exists; we just link to it).

   FILTERING + SORTING (Round 6 — SERVER-SIDE, Radzen LoadData mode):
     The grid runs in server-driven paging mode (same architecture as the
     Round-5 AdminUsers grid and the Round-4 Sales grid): Data + Count +
     LoadData. Radzen re-fires LoadData on mount, on pager clicks, on
     sort-header clicks, and on column-filter changes, and the page
     translates the descriptors into GetProductsPaginatedQuery fields
     (ProductsGridStateTranslator):
       - Name     → server-side text filter (LOWER()/LIKE in SQL)
       - Price    → numeric comparison filter on Price.Amount (complex
                    property — EF flattens it, same translation the
                    Round-4 shop sorts rely on)
       - Stock    → numeric comparison filter on StockQuantity
       - Status   → dropdown with colored tags (In stock=green,
                    Out of stock=red); the dropdown's culture-neutral key
                    travels as the filter descriptor's payload
       - Category / Sub-Category / Sub-Sub-Category → text filters on the
         RESOLVED display names; the query handler resolves the term
         against the category tree it already loads for DTO enrichment
         and pushes the matched Id sets into SQL as IN clauses (the
         product row stores only Guid FKs — see
         ProductsListFilters.CategoryIds). This keeps the page's existing
         free-text filter UX rather than swapping in a dropdown.
     Sorting is server-side too (Name asc/desc, Price asc/desc via the
     Round-4 keys, Stock asc/desc — each with a total tiebreaker). The
     three category columns are filterable but NOT sortable: their names
     live on the Category aggregate, so a sort would need a cross-table
     LEFT JOIN in the ORDER BY (the same trade-off documented for the
     users list's GroupName column).
     This removes the pre-Round-6 100-row ceiling: the page used to load
     PageSize=100 once (the handler's MaxPageSize cap) and filter/sort
     client-side — every product past the first 100 (name-ordered) was
     INVISIBLE to staff, and the pager described only those 100 rows.

   DEACTIVATION FLOW (matches the user's spec):
     1. User clicks the Deactivate icon (block icon, Danger color) on a row
        whose StockQuantity > 0.
     2. A Radzen dialog opens (DeactivateProductDialog.razor) explaining:
        "Deactivating this product means its stock becomes 0. If you still
        have some in stock but want it deactivated, write down the current
        stock number yourself — the system does not remember it. Confirm?"
        The dialog shows the current stock value for clarity.
     3. If the user clicks Confirm, DeactivateProductCommand is dispatched
        (sets StockQuantity to 0). On success: toast + grid.Reload() (the
        CURRENT page + filters reload in place — Round 6; the old full-list
        reload also reset every client-side filter). The row's Stock column
        now shows 0 in red, the Status column shows "Out of stock" (red
        badge), and the row itself is rendered at reduced opacity
        (RowRender hook).
     4. If the user clicks Cancel (or X / Esc), no command is dispatched.

     For products whose StockQuantity is already 0, the Deactivate button
     is HIDDEN (the product is already deactivated — there's nothing to do).
     A Restock button is shown instead so the user can reactivate.

   COLUMNS (in order — Actions is LAST; do NOT wrap any column in
   <AuthorizeView> because that re-orders columns at render time — see the
   comment block on the Sales.razor fix in this same session):
     1. Picture (60px, no filter, no sort)
     2. Name (text filter, clickable link to /Products/{Id})
     3. Price (numeric filter on Price.Amount, right-aligned tabular-nums)
     4. Stock (numeric filter, colored: green=in-stock, amber=low ≤10, red=out)
     5. Category (text filter, shows "(deactivated)" suffix if the referenced
        Category has been soft-deleted; NOT sortable — cross-aggregate)
     6. Sub-Category (text filter, "(deactivated)" suffix when applicable;
        em-dash when product has no sub-category; NOT sortable)
     7. Sub-Sub-Category (text filter, same pattern as Sub-Category;
        NOT sortable)
     8. Status (dropdown filter: All / In stock / Out of stock — colored
        badges; Out-of-stock = red, In-stock = green)
     9. Actions (View + Restock + Deactivate; last column; no filter)

   LOCALIZATION:
     All user-visible strings go through IStringLocalizer<AdminProducts>
     (Loc). Keys in AdminProducts.en-US.resx + AdminProducts.fa-IR.resx.

   ANTIFORGERY:
     Not needed — no EditForm. All button clicks are async event handlers
     that dispatch commands via Wolverine. The Blazor circuit's anti-forgery
     token is established at circuit start. *@

@page "/Admin/Products"
@* Policy: Policies.ProductManagement *@
@attribute [Authorize(Policy = "ProductManagement")]
@attribute [SuppressMessage("Usage", "BL0005:Component parameters should not be set outside their component", Justification = "Radzen exposes FilterValue/FilterOperator on RadzenDataGridColumn as the official programmatic filter API per https://radzen.com/documentation/blazor/data-grid-programmatic-filter/. The BL0005 analyzer (designed for plain Blazor component params) does not understand this Radzen-specific contract; the mutations are intentional and load-bearing.")]
@using System.Globalization
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization
@using Wolverine
@using TakOne.Application.Products.DTOs
@using TakOne.Application.Products.Queries.GetProductsPaginated
@using TakOne.Application.Products.Commands.IncreaseProductStock
@using TakOne.Application.Products.Commands.DeactivateProduct
@using TakOne.SharedKernel.Common
@using TakOne.SharedKernel.DTOs
@using TakOne.WebUI.Services
@using TakOne.WebUI.Components.Dialogs.DeactivateProductDialog
@using TakOne.WebUI.Components.Dialogs.RestockProductDialog
@inject IMessageBus MessageBus
@inject NavigationManager Navigation
@inject IStringLocalizer<AdminProducts> Loc
@inject ToastService Toast
@inject ErrorDisplayService ErrorDisplay
@inject Radzen.DialogService DialogService
@inject Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.ILogger<AdminProducts> Logger
@inject IJSRuntime JS

<PageTitle>@Loc["PageTitle"]</PageTitle>

<HeadContent>
    <style>
            /* Page header — mirrors AdminUsers.razor's .admin-header layout
                                               so the two pages read as members of the same family. */
            .admin-header {
                display: flex;
                align-items: center;
                justify-content: space-between;
                gap: 1rem;
                flex-wrap: wrap;
            }

            .product-thumb {
                width: 40px;
                height: 40px;
                display: flex;
                align-items: center;
                justify-content: center;
                background: var(--rz-base-background-color);
                border: 1px solid var(--rz-base-border-color);
                border-radius: 4px;
                overflow: hidden;
            }

                .product-thumb img {
                    width: 100%;
                    height: 100%;
                    object-fit: contain;
                }

                .product-thumb .rz-icon {
                    font-size: 1.4rem;
                    color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color);
                }

            .stock-cell {
                font-weight: 600;
            }

            .stock-cell-out {
                color: var(--rz-danger);
            }

            .stock-cell-low {
                color: var(--rz-warning);
            }

            .stock-cell-in {
                color: var(--rz-success);
            }

            /* Category-name cell — when the referenced category is deactivated,
                                       we render the name followed by a small red "(deactivated)" badge.
                                       The flex layout keeps the name + badge on one line and lets them
                                       wrap gracefully on narrow viewports. */
            .cat-cell {
                display: inline-flex;
                align-items: center;
                gap: 0.35rem;
                flex-wrap: wrap;
            }

            .cat-deactivated-badge {
                font-size: 0.7rem;
                font-weight: 600;
                color: var(--rz-danger);
                border: 1px solid var(--rz-danger);
                border-radius: 9999px;
                padding: 0.05rem 0.4rem;
                text-transform: uppercase;
                letter-spacing: 0.02em;
                opacity: 0.85;
            }

            .cat-empty {
                color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color);
                font-style: italic;
            }

            .row-actions {
                display: flex;
                gap: 0.25rem;
                justify-content: flex-end;
                align-items: center;
            }

            /* ── Loading veil (Round 6, mirrors the AdminUsers/Sales grids) ──
               Covers the MOUNTED grid while a server round trip is in
               flight. The grid must stay mounted underneath — unmounting
               it would discard its LoadData state and re-fire the initial
               load in a loop. First load (no data yet): the full skeleton
               rhythm; subsequent loads: a spinner glyph. */
            .admin-products-grid-wrap {
                position: relative;
            }

            .admin-products-loading-veil {
                position: absolute;
                inset: 0;
                z-index: 5;
                display: flex;
                align-items: flex-start;
                justify-content: center;
                padding-top: 3rem;
                background: var(--rz-base-background-color);
                opacity: 0.72;
            }

            .admin-products-loading-veil--skeleton {
                align-items: stretch;
                padding: 0.5rem;
                background: var(--rz-base-background-color);
            }

            .admin-products-loading-spin {
                font-size: 2.2rem;
                color: var(--rz-primary);
                animation: admin-products-spin 1.1s linear infinite;
            }

            @@keyframes admin-products-spin {
                to {
                    transform: rotate(360deg);
                }
            }

            /* Reduced motion: the spinner becomes a static glyph. */
            @@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
                .admin-products-loading-spin {
                    animation: none;
                }
            }
    </style>
</HeadContent>

<RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Vertical" Gap="1rem">
    @* ── Page header ── *@
    <div class="admin-header">
        <RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Horizontal" AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Gap="0.75rem">
            <RadzenIcon Icon="inventory" Style="font-size: 1.75rem; color: var(--rz-primary);" />
            <RadzenText Text="@Loc["PageHeading"]" TextStyle="TextStyle.H5" Style="font-weight: 600; margin: 0;" />
        </RadzenStack>

        <RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Horizontal" AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Gap="0.5rem">
            @* Export CSV (Round 6) — exports EVERY product matching the active
               filters (all pages, capped at 2,000) with the same filters +
               sort the grid shows. Previously it could only ship the ≤100
               loaded rows. Admin-only page ⇒ no extra authorization needed
               beyond the page's [Authorize]. *@
            <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["ExportCsvButton"]"
                          Icon="download"
                          ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Secondary"
                          Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                          Disabled="@(_isLoading || _isExporting || _totalCount == 0)"
                          Click="@ExportProductsCsvAsync">
                @if (_isExporting)
                {
                    <RadzenIcon Icon="hourglass_top" Style="margin-inline-start: 0.35rem;" />
                }
            </RadzenButton>
            <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["CreateProductButton"]"
                          Icon="add"
                          ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Primary"
                          Variant="Variant.Filled"
                          Click="@(() => Navigation.NavigateTo("/Admin/Products/Create", forceLoad: false))" />
        </RadzenStack>
    </div>

    @* ── Error banner ──
       On load failure the grid is replaced entirely: there is nothing
       meaningful to page through, and the Retry button re-mounts the
       grid (which re-fires LoadData from scratch). *@
    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(_loadError))
    {
        <RadzenAlert Title="@Loc["ErrorTitle"]"
                     Text="@_loadError"
                     AlertStyle="AlertStyle.Danger"
                     Variant="Variant.Filled"
                     ShowIcon="true">
            <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["RetryButton"]"
                          Icon="refresh"
                          ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Primary"
                          Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                          Click="@RetryLoadAsync"
                          Style="margin-top: 0.5rem;" />
        </RadzenAlert>
    }

    @* ── Empty state (no products at all, or none matching the filters) ──
       _totalCount is the SERVER-side count of every row matching the
       active filters — zero here means "no products exist for this filter
       combination". When NO filter is active and the count is zero, this
       is a fresh install with no products yet — the "create the first
       product" state. When a filter IS active and matches zero rows, a
       targeted message (plus the Clear button) beats a bare "no products
       found". *@
    else if (_totalCount == 0 && !_isLoading)
    {
        @if (HasActiveFilters)
        {
            <RadzenCard Variant="Variant.Filled" Style="padding: 3rem; text-align: center;">
                <RadzenStack AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Gap="1rem">
                    <RadzenIcon Icon="filter_alt_off"
                                Style="font-size: 3rem; color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color);" />
                    <RadzenText Text="@Loc["EmptyTitleFiltered"]"
                                TextStyle="TextStyle.H6" />
                    <RadzenText Text="@Loc["EmptyMessageFiltered"]"
                                Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); max-width: 360px;" />
                    <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["ClearFiltersButton"]"
                                  Icon="restart_alt"
                                  ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Secondary"
                                  Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                                  Click="@ClearAllFiltersAsync" />
                </RadzenStack>
            </RadzenCard>
        }
        else
        {
            <RadzenCard Variant="Variant.Filled" Style="padding: 3rem; text-align: center;">
                <RadzenStack AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Gap="1rem">
                    <RadzenIcon Icon="inventory_2"
                                Style="font-size: 3rem; color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color);" />
                    <RadzenText Text="@Loc["EmptyTitle"]"
                                TextStyle="TextStyle.H6" />
                    <RadzenText Text="@Loc["EmptyMessage"]"
                                Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); max-width: 360px;" />
                    <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["CreateFirstProductButton"]"
                                  Icon="add"
                                  ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Primary"
                                  Variant="Variant.Filled"
                                  Click="@(() => Navigation.NavigateTo("/Admin/Products/Create", forceLoad: false))" />
                </RadzenStack>
            </RadzenCard>
        }
    }

    @* ── Product table (Round 6 — Radzen LoadData, server-driven) ──
       Data + Count + LoadData: the grid fires LoadData on mount, on pager
       clicks, on sort-header clicks, and on filter changes; the page
       translates the descriptors into the typed query. The grid stays
       MOUNTED under the loading veil (unmounting would discard its
       LoadData state and re-fire the initial load in a loop). Name,
       Price, Stock and Status sort server-side; the three category
       columns are deliberately NOT sortable (the names live on another
       aggregate — see the ProductSortBy doc). The grid uses
       AllowColumnReorder="false" so the source-order columns are respected
       (defense against stale persisted reorder state from a prior session).
       IMPORTANT: do NOT wrap any <RadzenDataGridColumn> in <AuthorizeView> —
       that re-orders columns at render time. See Sales.razor's comment for
       the full root-cause explanation. *@
    else
    {
        <div class="admin-products-grid-wrap">
            <RadzenCard Variant="Variant.Filled" Class="admin-products-grid" Style="padding: 0.5rem;">
            <RadzenDataGrid @ref="grid"
                            Data="@_products"
                            Count="@_totalCount"
                            LoadData="@LoadGridDataAsync"
                            TItem="ProductListItemDto"
                            AllowSorting="true"
                            GotoFirstPageOnSort="true"
                            AllowFiltering="true"
                            FilterMode="FilterMode.SimpleWithMenu"
                            FilterCaseSensitivity="FilterCaseSensitivity.CaseInsensitive"
                            AllowPaging="true"
                            PageSize="20"
                            PagerAlwaysVisible="true"
                            PageSizeOptions="@(new[] { 20, 50, 100 })"
                            PageSizeText="@Loc["PagerPageSizeText"]"
                            PagerHorizontalAlign="HorizontalAlign.Center"
                            ShowPagingSummary="true"
                            PagingSummaryFormat="@Loc["PagerSummaryFormat"]"
                            EmptyText="@Loc["EmptyText"]"
                            IsLoading="@(_isLoading && _products is { Count: > 0 })"
                            AllowColumnReorder="false"
                            Style="width: 100%;"
                            RowRender="@OnRowRender">
                <Columns>

                    @* ── Picture — no filter, no sort, fixed width ── *@
                    <RadzenDataGridColumn TItem="ProductListItemDto"
                                          Width="60px"
                                          Sortable="false"
                                          Filterable="false">
                        <Template Context="product">
                            <div class="product-thumb">
                                @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(product.PictureUrl))
                                {
                                    <img src="@product.PictureUrl" alt="@product.Name" loading="lazy" />
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <RadzenIcon Icon="image" />
                                }
                            </div>
                        </Template>
                    </RadzenDataGridColumn>

                    @* ── Name — text filter (Radzen default, translated
                           server-side), clickable link to /Products/{Id} ── *@
                    <RadzenDataGridColumn TItem="ProductListItemDto"
                                          Property="Name"
                                          Title="@Loc["ColName"]"
                                          Width="*">
                        <Template Context="product">
                            <RadzenLink Text="@product.Name"
                                        Path="@($"/Products/{product.Id}")"
                                        Style="font-weight: 600;" />
                        </Template>
                    </RadzenDataGridColumn>

                    @* ── Price — numeric filter on Price.Amount, right-aligned ──
                       The complex-property path is what Radzen emits in the
                       filter/sort descriptors; the translator maps it to the
                       typed numeric filter + the Round-4 price sort keys. *@
                    <RadzenDataGridColumn TItem="ProductListItemDto"
                                          Property="Price.Amount"
                                          Title="@Loc["ColPrice"]"
                                          Width="140px"
                                          TextAlign="TextAlign.Right">
                        <Template Context="product">
                            <span style="font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;">@FormatPrice(product.Price)</span>
                        </Template>
                    </RadzenDataGridColumn>

                    @* ── Stock — numeric filter, color-coded (red=0, amber ≤10, green >10) ──
                       NOTE: this column shares Property="StockQuantity" with
                       the Status column below. Their filter descriptors are
                       told apart by payload shape (number vs the status
                       dropdown's string key) — see
                       ProductsGridStateTranslator's class remarks. Both
                       filters can be active at once and AND in SQL. *@
                    <RadzenDataGridColumn TItem="ProductListItemDto"
                                          Property="StockQuantity"
                                          Title="@Loc["ColStock"]"
                                          Width="100px"
                                          TextAlign="TextAlign.Right">
                        <Template Context="product">
                            <span class="stock-cell @GetStockClass(product.StockQuantity)">
                                @(CultureFormat.FormatNumber(product.StockQuantity))
                            </span>
                        </Template>
                    </RadzenDataGridColumn>

                    @* ── Category — text filter (resolved to a CategoryId set
                           server-side), "(deactivated)" badge when
                           CategoryIsActive=false, NOT sortable ──
                       The Property is bound to CategoryName so Radzen's text
                       filter operates on the resolved display name. The
                       deactivated badge is appended in the Template; it's not
                       part of the filter value. Sortable=false: the name
                       lives on the Category aggregate (no navigation
                       property on Product), so a sort would need a
                       cross-table LEFT JOIN in the ORDER BY — deferred, see
                       the ProductSortBy doc comment. *@
                    <RadzenDataGridColumn TItem="ProductListItemDto"
                                          Property="CategoryName"
                                          Title="@Loc["ColCategory"]"
                                          Width="200px"
                                          Sortable="false">
                        <Template Context="product">
                            <span class="cat-cell">
                                @if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(product.CategoryName))
                                {
                                    <span class="cat-empty">@Loc["UnknownCategory"]</span>
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <span>@product.CategoryName</span>
                                }
                                @if (!product.CategoryIsActive)
                                {
                                    <span class="cat-deactivated-badge">@Loc["DeactivatedSuffix"]</span>
                                }
                            </span>
                        </Template>
                    </RadzenDataGridColumn>

                    @* ── Sub-Category — text filter, em-dash when null,
                           "(deactivated)" when inactive, NOT sortable ── *@
                    <RadzenDataGridColumn TItem="ProductListItemDto"
                                          Property="SubCategoryName"
                                          Title="@Loc["ColSubCategory"]"
                                          Width="200px"
                                          Sortable="false">
                        <Template Context="product">
                            <span class="cat-cell">
                                @if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(product.SubCategoryName))
                                {
                                    <span class="cat-empty">@Loc["NoSubCategory"]</span>
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <span>@product.SubCategoryName</span>
                                }
                                @if (!product.SubCategoryIsActive)
                                {
                                    <span class="cat-deactivated-badge">@Loc["DeactivatedSuffix"]</span>
                                }
                            </span>
                        </Template>
                    </RadzenDataGridColumn>

                    @* ── Sub-Sub-Category — text filter, em-dash when null,
                           "(deactivated)" when inactive, NOT sortable ── *@
                    <RadzenDataGridColumn TItem="ProductListItemDto"
                                          Property="SubSubCategoryName"
                                          Title="@Loc["ColSubSubCategory"]"
                                          Width="200px"
                                          Sortable="false">
                        <Template Context="product">
                            <span class="cat-cell">
                                @if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(product.SubSubCategoryName))
                                {
                                    <span class="cat-empty">@Loc["NoSubSubCategory"]</span>
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <span>@product.SubSubCategoryName</span>
                                }
                                @if (!product.SubSubCategoryIsActive)
                                {
                                    <span class="cat-deactivated-badge">@Loc["DeactivatedSuffix"]</span>
                                }
                            </span>
                        </Template>
                    </RadzenDataGridColumn>

                    @* ── Status — dropdown filter with colored badges ──
                       Two-state filter mirroring AdminUsers' Status column:
                       "In stock" (StockQuantity > 0, green badge) vs
                       "Out of stock" (StockQuantity == 0, red badge).
                       Round 6: the dropdown writes its culture-neutral
                       string key ("InStock"/"OutOfStock") into the column's
                       programmatic FilterValue — the key travels as the
                       descriptor payload (the same pattern as the Sales
                       page's status column) and the translator maps it to
                       the typed ProductStockStatus filter. The pre-Round-6
                       GreaterThanOrEquals+1 dance existed to dodge Radzen's
                       0-value sentinel on the BUILT-IN filter path; with a
                       custom FilterTemplate + LoadData mode the key is the
                       only thing that travels, so the sentinel is gone.
                       AllowClear resets to null (All). *@
                    <RadzenDataGridColumn TItem="ProductListItemDto"
                                          Property="StockQuantity"
                                          Title="@Loc["ColStatus"]"
                                          Width="140px"
                                          TextAlign="TextAlign.Center"
                                          FilterOperator="FilterOperator.Equals">
                        <Template Context="product">
                            <RadzenBadge Text="@GetStatusLabel(product.StockQuantity > 0)"
                                         BadgeStyle="@GetStatusBadgeStyle(product.StockQuantity > 0)"
                                         IsPill="true" />
                        </Template>
                        <FilterTemplate Context="column">
                            <RadzenDropDown @bind-Value="_selectedStatusFilter"
                                            Data="@_statusFilterOptions"
                                            TValue="string"
                                            AllowClear="true"
                                            Placeholder="@Loc["StatusAll"]"
                                            Change="@(args => OnStatusFilterChanged(_selectedStatusFilter, column))"
                                            Style="width: 100%; min-width: 130px;">
                                <ValueTemplate Context="selected">
                                    @{
                                        var selectedStr = selected?.ToString();
                                    }
                                    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(selectedStr))
                                    {
                                        <RadzenBadge Text="@GetStatusLabel(selectedStr)"
                                                     BadgeStyle="@GetStatusBadgeStyle(selectedStr)"
                                                     IsPill="true" />
                                    }
                                    else
                                    {
                                        <span>@Loc["StatusAll"]</span>
                                    }
                                </ValueTemplate>
                                <Template Context="statusOption">
                                    @{
                                        var optStr = statusOption?.ToString();
                                    }
                                    <RadzenBadge Text="@GetStatusLabel(optStr)"
                                                 BadgeStyle="@GetStatusBadgeStyle(optStr)"
                                                 IsPill="true" />
                                </Template>
                            </RadzenDropDown>
                        </FilterTemplate>
                    </RadzenDataGridColumn>

                    @* ── Actions — View + Restock + Deactivate (last column; no filter) ──
                       Three icon-only text buttons:
                       - visibility (Info) → navigates to /Products/{Id}
                       - add_circle_outline (Success) → opens Restock prompt
                         (always available — restocking an already-in-stock
                         product just adds more)
                       - block (Danger) → opens DeactivateProductDialog ONLY
                         when StockQuantity > 0. When StockQuantity is already
                         0, the Deactivate button is HIDDEN (the product is
                         already deactivated; Restock is the only action). *@
                    <RadzenDataGridColumn TItem="ProductListItemDto"
                                          Title="@Loc["ColActions"]"
                                          Width="160px"
                                          TextAlign="TextAlign.Center"
                                          Sortable="false"
                                          Filterable="false">
                        <Template Context="product">
                            <div class="row-actions">
                                <RadzenButton Icon="visibility"
                                              Variant="Variant.Text"
                                              ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Info"
                                              title="@Loc["ActionView"]"
                                              aria-label="@Loc["ActionView"]"
                                              Click="@(() => Navigation.NavigateTo($"/Products/{product.Id}", forceLoad: false))" />

                                <RadzenButton Icon="add_circle_outline"
                                              Variant="Variant.Text"
                                              ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Success"
                                              title="@Loc["ActionRestock"]"
                                              aria-label="@Loc["ActionRestock"]"
                                              Disabled="@(_isBusyProductId == product.Id)"
                                              Click="@(() => HandleRestockAsync(product))" />

                                @if (product.StockQuantity > 0)
                                {
                                    <RadzenButton Icon="block"
                                                  Variant="Variant.Text"
                                                  ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Danger"
                                                  title="@Loc["ActionDeactivate"]"
                                                  aria-label="@Loc["ActionDeactivate"]"
                                                  Disabled="@(_isBusyProductId == product.Id)"
                                                  Click="@(() => HandleDeactivateAsync(product))" />
                                }
                            </div>
                        </Template>
                    </RadzenDataGridColumn>
                </Columns>
            </RadzenDataGrid>
            </RadzenCard>

            @* ── Loading veil (Round 6) ──
               Covers the mounted grid while a server round trip is in
               flight. First load (no data yet): the full skeleton rhythm
               (tm-skel utilities from app.css — theme-adaptive,
               reduced-motion aware; the column rhythm mirrors the product
               grid: thumb, name, price, stock, 3 category columns, status,
               actions). Subsequent loads: a spinner glyph. The grid MUST
               stay mounted underneath — unmounting it would discard its
               LoadData state and re-fire the initial load in a loop. *@
            @if (_isLoading)
            {
                @if (_products is not { Count: > 0 })
                {
                    <div class="admin-products-loading-veil admin-products-loading-veil--skeleton"
                         role="status"
                         aria-live="polite"
                         aria-label="@Loc["LoadingAriaLabel"]">
                        <div class="tm-skel-stack" style="padding: 0.5rem; width: 100%;">
                            @for (var i = 0; i < 8; i++)
                            {
                                <div class="tm-skel-grid" style="--tm-skel-cols: 0.6fr 1.6fr 0.7fr 0.5fr 1fr 1fr 1fr 0.7fr 0.8fr;">
                                    <div class="tm-skel tm-skel--thumb"></div>
                                    <div class="tm-skel tm-skel--line"></div>
                                    <div class="tm-skel tm-skel--line tm-skel--w60"></div>
                                    <div class="tm-skel tm-skel--line tm-skel--w40"></div>
                                    <div class="tm-skel tm-skel--line tm-skel--w80"></div>
                                    <div class="tm-skel tm-skel--line tm-skel--w80"></div>
                                    <div class="tm-skel tm-skel--line tm-skel--w80"></div>
                                    <div class="tm-skel tm-skel--chip"></div>
                                    <div class="tm-skel tm-skel--chip"></div>
                                </div>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>
                }
                else
                {
                    <div class="admin-products-loading-veil" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-label="@Loc["LoadingAriaLabel"]">
                        <RadzenIcon Icon="progress_activity" Class="admin-products-loading-spin" />
                    </div>
                }
            }
        </div>
    }
</RadzenStack>

@code {
    // ── State ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// The CURRENT PAGE of products returned by the backend (Round 6:
    /// server-driven paging — exactly one page, not the pre-Round-6
    /// "first 100 products" client-side working set).
    ///
    /// NULL until the first load completes: Radzen only fires LoadData
    /// on mount when <c>Data</c> is null (binding an empty list at mount
    /// suppresses the initial load — pinned by the contract probes).
    /// Paths that REMOUNT the grid (retry after error, the empty state's
    /// Clear-all) reset this to null so the fresh mount fires its
    /// initial load.
    /// </summary>
    private List<ProductListItemDto>? _products;

    /// <summary>
    /// The SERVER-SIDE total count of rows matching the active filters
    /// (drives the grid's Count parameter → pager page count + summary).
    /// </summary>
    private int _totalCount;

    private RadzenDataGrid<ProductListItemDto> grid = default!;

    // Status filter state — null = "no filter" (AllowClear=true on the
    // RadzenDropDown passes null when the user clears). String keys
    // "InStock"/"OutOfStock" (culture-neutral); the display text comes
    // from GetStatusLabel(string).
    private string? _selectedStatusFilter;

    // Status filter options — string keys "InStock"/"OutOfStock"
    // (culture-neutral). The display text comes from GetStatusLabel(string).
    private readonly List<string> _statusFilterOptions = new()
    {
        "InStock", "OutOfStock"
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// Loading/error state. _isLoading starts TRUE: the grid mounts under
    /// the skeleton veil and fires its initial LoadData, which flips it to
    /// false on completion.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _isLoading = true;
    private string _loadError = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// True while the CSV export's multi-page fetch loop is running —
    /// disables the export button so double-clicks can't race two loops.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _isExporting;

    // ── Round 6 server-side filter/sort state (translated from the
    //    grid's LoadDataArgs on every load; also reused by the CSV
    //    export so it ships exactly what the filters select) ──────

    private ProductsTextFilter? _activeNameFilter;
    private ProductStockStatus? _activeStockStatus;
    private ProductsNumberFilter? _activePriceFilter;
    private ProductsNumberFilter? _activeStockFilter;
    private ProductsTextFilter? _activeCategoryNameFilter;
    private ProductsTextFilter? _activeSubCategoryNameFilter;
    private ProductsTextFilter? _activeSubSubCategoryNameFilter;
    private ProductSortBy? _activeSortBy;
    private bool _activeSortDescending;

    /// <summary>
    /// True when ANY server-side column filter is active — drives the
    /// filtered-empty state's copy and the one-click Clear-all button.
    /// (The sort is deliberately not "active filter" state: sorting
    /// never changes WHICH rows match, so it must not flip the empty
    /// state's variant.)
    /// </summary>
    private bool HasActiveFilters =>
        _activeNameFilter is not null ||
        _activeStockStatus is not null ||
        _activePriceFilter is not null ||
        _activeStockFilter is not null ||
        _activeCategoryNameFilter is not null ||
        _activeSubCategoryNameFilter is not null ||
        _activeSubSubCategoryNameFilter is not null;

    /// <summary>
    /// Re-entrancy guard for the out-of-range auto-heal (see
    /// LoadGridDataAsync): a heal fires grid.FirstPage(true), which
    /// re-enters LoadGridDataAsync — the guard keeps a pathological
    /// sequence of loads from healing recursively.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _healingPage;

    /// <summary>
    /// The Id of the product whose action button is currently in flight.
    /// Null when no action is pending. Used to disable that row's action
    /// buttons while the round-trip is in progress (prevents double-clicks).
    /// </summary>
    private Guid? _isBusyProductId;

    // ── Lifecycle ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    // No OnInitializedAsync: there is no pre-grid work to do (the status
    // filter options are static, and no lookup lists need loading — the
    // category-name filters resolve server-side against the handler's own
    // category-tree load). The data load is NOT kicked off here either —
    // the grid mounts in the first render and fires its LoadData-on-init,
    // which drives LoadGridDataAsync. Kicking a manual load here too would
    // double the initial query.

    // ── Data loading (Radzen LoadData — Round 6) ────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// The grid's LoadData callback: translates Radzen's descriptors into
    /// the typed query fields (ProductsGridStateTranslator), dispatches, and
    /// hands the page back via Data + Count. Fired on mount, on pager
    /// clicks, on sort-header clicks, and on column-filter changes.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task LoadGridDataAsync(LoadDataArgs args)
    {
        _isLoading = true;
        _loadError = string.Empty;

        try
        {
            (_activeSortBy, _activeSortDescending) = ProductsGridStateTranslator.TranslateSort(args.Sorts);
            var columnFilters = ProductsGridStateTranslator.TranslateFilters(args.Filters);
            _activeNameFilter = columnFilters.Name;
            _activeStockStatus = columnFilters.StockStatus;
            _activePriceFilter = columnFilters.Price;
            _activeStockFilter = columnFilters.Stock;
            _activeCategoryNameFilter = columnFilters.CategoryName;
            _activeSubCategoryNameFilter = columnFilters.SubCategoryName;
            _activeSubSubCategoryNameFilter = columnFilters.SubSubCategoryName;

            var pageSize = args.Top is > 0 ? args.Top.Value : 20;
            var pageNumber = (args.Skip ?? 0) / pageSize + 1;

            var result = await MessageBus.InvokeAsync<PaginatedResult<ProductListItemDto>>(
                BuildQuery(pageNumber, pageSize));

            _products = result.Items.ToList();
            _totalCount = result.TotalCount;

            // OUT-OF-RANGE AUTO-HEAL: if the filters shrank the result set
            // while the grid sat on a high page (the built-in filter-row
            // inputs reload with the CURRENT page offset), the loaded page
            // is empty but the total is not — jump back to page 1 instead
            // of showing a mystifying blank page. FirstPage(true) re-fires
            // LoadData with Skip=0; the heal guard prevents recursion.
            if (!_healingPage &&
                _products.Count == 0 &&
                _totalCount > 0 &&
                (args.Skip ?? 0) >= _totalCount)
            {
                _healingPage = true;
                try
                {
                    if (grid is not null)
                    {
                        await grid.FirstPage(true);
                        return; // the re-fired load owns the state now
                    }
                }
                finally
                {
                    _healingPage = false;
                }
            }
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            _loadError = ErrorDisplay.Unexpected(UnexpectedErrorCodes.AdminProducts_LoadFailure);
            _products = null; // the retry remount must fire LoadData again
            _totalCount = 0;
        }
        finally
        {
            _isLoading = false;
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Builds the typed query from the page-level filter/sort state (kept
    /// current by <see cref="ProductsGridStateTranslator"/> on every load).
    /// Shared by the grid load and the CSV export so the export ships
    /// exactly what the filters select. IncludeInactive=true: this is the
    /// staff view — deactivated (zero-stock) products must be visible (the
    /// handler re-verifies the caller's Admin/Manager role anyway).
    /// </summary>
    private GetProductsPaginatedQuery BuildQuery(int pageNumber, int pageSize) => new()
    {
        PageNumber = pageNumber,
        PageSize = pageSize,
        IncludeInactive = true,
        NameFilter = _activeNameFilter,
        StockStatus = _activeStockStatus,
        PriceFilter = _activePriceFilter,
        StockFilter = _activeStockFilter,
        CategoryNameFilter = _activeCategoryNameFilter,
        SubCategoryNameFilter = _activeSubCategoryNameFilter,
        SubSubCategoryNameFilter = _activeSubSubCategoryNameFilter,
        SortBy = _activeSortBy,
        SortDescending = _activeSortDescending
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// Retry after a load error: clears the error so the grid branch
    /// re-renders — the grid re-mounts and its LoadData-on-init performs
    /// the fresh load.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task RetryLoadAsync()
    {
        _loadError = string.Empty;
        _isLoading = true;
        _products = null; // Data=null at remount → the grid fires LoadData
        await Task.CompletedTask;
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Clears EVERY column filter, then reloads. The filtered-empty
    /// state's one-click escape hatch. This button only renders when the
    /// grid is UNMOUNTED (the empty state replaced it), so the reload
    /// happens via REMOUNT: the grid mounts with Data=null and fires its
    /// LoadData-on-init with every filter naturally reset (fresh column
    /// instances carry no filter values). Do NOT call grid.FirstPage(true)
    /// here — the disposed grid's methods must not be touched.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ClearAllFiltersAsync()
    {
        _selectedStatusFilter = null;

        // REMOUNT TRIGGER: _products=null makes the grid mount with Data=null
        // (which fires LoadData — the pinned Radzen contract), and
        // _isLoading=true keeps the grid branch rendered (instead of
        // flashing the empty state) until the fresh load completes.
        _products = null;
        _isLoading = true;

        await Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    // ── Filter handlers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// The status dropdown's Change handler: sets the column's
    /// programmatic filter value (Radzen's documented API — same
    /// BL0005-suppressed contract as Sales/AdminUsers), then restarts
    /// from PAGE 1 via grid.FirstPage(true): a status filter narrows the
    /// result set, and page N of the wider set is usually past the end of
    /// the narrower one. The bound field is updated by @bind-Value before
    /// the Change event fires, so we pass the field (not the args
    /// parameter) to the handler.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task OnStatusFilterChanged(string? value, RadzenDataGridColumn<ProductListItemDto> column)
    {
        _selectedStatusFilter = value;

        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(value))
        {
            column.FilterValue = null;
        }
        else
        {
            // The culture-neutral string key travels as the descriptor
            // payload; the translator maps "InStock"/"OutOfStock" to the
            // typed ProductStockStatus filter (see the column's markup
            // comment for why the old GreaterThanOrEquals+1 sentinel
            // dance is gone).
            column.FilterValue = value;
            column.FilterOperator = FilterOperator.Equals;
        }

        await grid.FirstPage(true);
    }

    // ── Restock ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Opens the RestockProductDialog (a Radzen dialog component) so the
    /// user can enter the quantity to add to the product's stock. On
    /// Confirm, dispatches <see cref="IncreaseProductStockCommand"/>.
    ///
    /// WHY A DIALOG (not JS prompt()):
    ///   The user explicitly asked for a "proper popup" — the previous
    ///   implementation used the browser's native prompt(), which is
    ///   ugly, unstyled, un-themeable, and doesn't render localized
    ///   text correctly in some browsers. The custom dialog gives us:
    ///     1. The product name (so the user knows which product
    ///        they're restocking without scrolling back to the grid).
    ///     2. The current stock value (so the user can see how much
    ///        is already there before deciding how much to add).
    ///     3. A RadzenNumeric input with Min=1, Step=1, spinner
    ///        arrows — much nicer UX than a free-text JS prompt.
    ///     4. Inline validation in the dialog itself (no need to
    ///        Toast.Warning on bad input — the dialog handles it).
    ///   The dialog returns a positive int if the user confirmed, or
    ///   null/false otherwise.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task HandleRestockAsync(ProductListItemDto product)
    {
        if (_isBusyProductId.HasValue) return;

        object? result;
        try
        {
            result = await DialogService.OpenAsync<RestockProductDialog>(
                Loc["RestockDialogTitle"],
                new Dictionary<string, object?>
                {
                    { "ProductName", product.Name },
                    { "CurrentStock", product.StockQuantity }
                },
                new Radzen.DialogOptions
                {
                    Width = "460px",
                    Resizable = false,
                    Draggable = true,
                    CloseDialogOnOverlayClick = false,  // force the user to click a button — no accidental close
                    ShowClose = true                    // keep the X button so the user has an obvious escape
                });
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            // v4 #15: Console.WriteLine → structured ILogger pipeline. The
            // ILogger<T> is injected at the top of the file (matches the
            // SaleDetail.razor convention).
            Logger.LogError(ex, "Restock dialog open failed for product {ProductId}", product.Id);
            await Toast.UnexpectedError(UnexpectedErrorCodes.AdminProducts_RestockDialogOpen, summary: Loc["ErrorTitle"]);
            return;
        }

        // DialogService.Close(object) boxes the int. Anything that isn't
        // a positive int is treated as "user aborted" (covers null,
        // false, dismissed-via-X/Esc cases). The dialog itself already
        // validates that the entered value is a positive int before
        // calling Close(value), so we don't need to re-validate here.
        if (result is not int quantity || quantity <= 0) return;

        _isBusyProductId = product.Id;
        try
        {
            var command = new IncreaseProductStockCommand(product.Id, quantity);
            var cmdResult = await MessageBus.InvokeAsync<Result>(command);

            if (cmdResult.IsSuccess)
            {
                await Toast.Success(
                    string.Format(CultureInfo.CurrentCulture, Loc["RestockSuccess"], quantity, product.Name),
                    summary: Loc["RestockSuccessTitle"]);
                // Refresh the CURRENT page (Round 6): grid.Reload() re-fires
                // LoadData with the current page/filters — the row's stock
                // + status badge update in place without losing the user's
                // place. (The old full-list reload also reset every
                // client-side filter.)
                await grid.Reload();
            }
            else
            {
                await Toast.Error(ErrorDisplay.Localize(cmdResult.Error) ?? Loc["RestockError"], summary: Loc["ErrorTitle"]);
            }
        }
        catch
        {
            await Toast.UnexpectedError(UnexpectedErrorCodes.AdminProducts_RestockFailure, summary: Loc["ErrorTitle"]);
        }
        finally
        {
            _isBusyProductId = null;
        }
    }

    // ── Deactivate ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Opens the DeactivateProductDialog (a Radzen dialog component) that
    /// warns the user about the stock-loss implication and asks for
    /// confirmation. On Confirm, dispatches DeactivateProductCommand.
    ///
    /// WHY A DIALOG (not JS confirm()):
    ///   The user's spec explicitly asks for a popup that EXPLAINS the
    ///   implication ("deactivating means stock becomes 0; if you still
    ///   have inventory, write down the current stock number yourself")
    ///   and shows the current stock value for clarity. JS confirm() is
    ///   too sparse for that — we need a custom dialog with formatted
    ///   text + the current stock value. The dialog returns true if the
    ///   user confirmed, false/null if they cancelled.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task HandleDeactivateAsync(ProductListItemDto product)
    {
        if (_isBusyProductId.HasValue) return;

        // Guard: only show the dialog if there's stock to lose. The
        // markup also hides the button when StockQuantity == 0, but
        // defense-in-depth never hurts.
        if (product.StockQuantity <= 0)
        {
            await Toast.Warning(Loc["AlreadyDeactivated"], summary: Loc["ErrorTitle"]);
            return;
        }

        object? confirmed;
        try
        {
            confirmed = await DialogService.OpenAsync<DeactivateProductDialog>(
                Loc["DeactivateDialogTitle"],
                new Dictionary<string, object?>
                {
                    { "ProductName", product.Name },
                    { "CurrentStock", product.StockQuantity }
                },
                new Radzen.DialogOptions
                {
                    Width = "460px",
                    Resizable = false,
                    Draggable = true,
                    CloseDialogOnOverlayClick = false,  // force the user to click a button — no accidental close
                    ShowClose = true                    // keep the X button so the user has an obvious escape
                });
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            // v4 #15: Console.WriteLine → structured ILogger pipeline.
            Logger.LogError(ex, "Deactivate dialog open failed for product {ProductId}", product.Id);
            await Toast.UnexpectedError(UnexpectedErrorCodes.AdminProducts_DeactivateDialogOpen, summary: Loc["ErrorTitle"]);
            return;
        }

        // DialogService.Close(object) boxes a bool. Anything that isn't
        // exactly `true` is treated as "user aborted" (covers false, null,
        // and dismissed-via-X/Esc cases).
        if (confirmed is not true) return;

        _isBusyProductId = product.Id;
        try
        {
            var result = await MessageBus.InvokeAsync<Result>(
                new DeactivateProductCommand(product.Id));

            if (result.IsSuccess)
            {
                await Toast.Success(
                    string.Format(CultureInfo.CurrentCulture, Loc["DeactivateSuccess"], product.Name),
                    summary: Loc["DeactivateSuccessTitle"]);
                // Refresh the CURRENT page (Round 6 — see HandleRestockAsync).
                await grid.Reload();
            }
            else
            {
                await Toast.Error(ErrorDisplay.Localize(result.Error) ?? Loc["DeactivateError"], summary: Loc["ErrorTitle"]);
            }
        }
        catch
        {
            await Toast.UnexpectedError(UnexpectedErrorCodes.AdminProducts_DeactivateFailure, summary: Loc["ErrorTitle"]);
        }
        finally
        {
            _isBusyProductId = null;
        }
    }

    // ── Row render hook ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

    private void OnRowRender(RowRenderEventArgs<ProductListItemDto> args)
    {
        // Subtle visual cue for out-of-stock rows: slight opacity.
        // Mirrors AdminUsers.razor's OnRowRender for inactive users.
        // Per-row coloring beyond this is handled by the stock-cell CSS
        // class on the stock column itself.
        if (args.Data is { } p && p.StockQuantity == 0)
        {
            args.Attributes.Add("style", "opacity: 0.65;");
        }
    }

    // ── Display helpers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Formats a MoneyDto for display. IRR is labelled "ریال" (no division —
    /// admin pages show the real Rial amount stored in the database).
    /// Other currencies pass through unchanged.
    /// Delegates to <see cref="CultureFormat.FormatMoneyRial"/> for
    /// culture-native digits (Persian digits in fa-IR mode, ASCII in en-US).
    /// </summary>
    private static string FormatPrice(MoneyDto? price)
        => CultureFormat.FormatMoneyRial(price);

    private static string GetStockClass(int stock)
    {
        if (stock == 0) return "stock-cell-out";
        if (stock <= 10) return "stock-cell-low";
        return "stock-cell-in";
    }

    // ── Status helpers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Used by both the cell Template (bool overload — stock > 0) and the
    // filter dropdown's ValueTemplate/Template (string overload —
    // "InStock"/"OutOfStock" keys).

    private string GetStatusLabel(bool inStock)
        => inStock ? Loc["StatusInStockBadge"] : Loc["StatusOutOfStockBadge"];

    private string GetStatusLabel(string? status)
        => status switch
        {
            "InStock" => Loc["StatusInStock"],
            "OutOfStock" => Loc["StatusOutOfStock"],
            _ => Loc["StatusAll"]
        };

    private BadgeStyle GetStatusBadgeStyle(bool inStock)
        => inStock ? BadgeStyle.Success : BadgeStyle.Danger;

    private BadgeStyle GetStatusBadgeStyle(string? status)
        => status switch
        {
            "InStock" => BadgeStyle.Success,
            "OutOfStock" => BadgeStyle.Danger,
            _ => BadgeStyle.Base
        };

    // ── CSV export (Round 6 — all filtered rows, capped) ─────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Hard cap on exported rows: generous enough for any real export,
    /// small enough that a pathological filter can't hang the Blazor
    /// circuit building a giant string (same value as the Sales/Users
    /// exports).
    /// </summary>
    private const int ExportRowCap = 2000;

    /// <summary>
    /// Builds a CSV of EVERY product matching the active filters (all
    /// pages — a genuine improvement over the pre-Round-6 export, which
    /// could only ship the ≤100 loaded rows), capped at
    /// <see cref="ExportRowCap"/>, and downloads it via the takDownload
    /// helper. Column shape mirrors the grid: name, full category path,
    /// price (+currency as its own column), stock, and in/out-of-stock
    /// status. See Sales.razor's ExportSalesCsvAsync for the format
    /// rationale (RFC 4180 + invariant numbers + BOM-at-save).
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ExportProductsCsvAsync()
    {
        if (_totalCount == 0 || _isExporting)
        {
            return; // the button is disabled for empty lists — belt + braces
        }

        _isExporting = true;
        try
        {
            var builder = new CsvBuilder();
            builder.AddHeader(
                Loc["ColName"],
                Loc["ColCategory"],
                Loc["ColSubCategory"],
                Loc["ColSubSubCategory"],
                Loc["ColPrice"],
                Loc["ColCurrency"],
                Loc["ColStock"],
                Loc["ColStatus"]);

            var exported = 0;
            var pageNumber = 1;
            const int exportPageSize = 100; // the backend's MaxPageSize cap

            while (exported < ExportRowCap)
            {
                var result = await MessageBus.InvokeAsync<PaginatedResult<ProductListItemDto>>(
                    BuildQuery(pageNumber, exportPageSize)); // SAME active filters + sort as the grid

                foreach (var product in result.Items)
                {
                    if (exported >= ExportRowCap) break;

                    builder.AddRow(
                        product.Name,
                        AppendDeactivated(product.CategoryName, product.CategoryIsActive),
                        AppendDeactivated(product.SubCategoryName, product.SubCategoryIsActive),
                        AppendDeactivated(product.SubSubCategoryName, product.SubSubCategoryIsActive),
                        product.Price?.Amount,
                        product.Price?.Currency,
                        product.StockQuantity,
                        GetStatusLabel(product.StockQuantity > 0));
                    exported++;
                }

                // Last page reached when the server returns fewer rows
                // than requested.
                if (result.Items.Count < exportPageSize) break;
                pageNumber++;
            }

            var fileName = $"takone-products-{DateTime.UtcNow:yyyyMMdd-HHmmss}.csv";
            await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("takDownload.csv", fileName, builder.ToString());

            await Toast.Success(Loc["ExportCsvDone", exported], summary: Loc["PageHeading"]);
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // JS interop can fail after a circuit reconnect — a visible
            // toast beats a silently dead button.
            await Toast.Warning(Loc["ExportCsvError"], summary: Loc["ErrorTitle"]);
        }
        finally
        {
            _isExporting = false;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Renders a category cell for the export: the name, suffixed with a
    /// marker when the category is deactivated (mirrors the grid's
    /// "(deactivated)" badge so the export doesn't hide deactivations).
    /// </summary>
    private string AppendDeactivated(string name, bool isActive)
        => isActive || string.IsNullOrEmpty(name) ? name : $"{name} ({Loc["DeactivatedSuffix"]})";
}
